# Diff Explanation — <change-id>

Write this artifact in the configured report language. Keep code tokens, identifiers, paths, commands, table names, and conventional technical terms exact.

## What changed

Start with a short description of the behavior that is different after this change. A developer should understand the result before reading implementation details.

## Why it changed

Explain the concrete problem this change solves and why the chosen behavior fixes it.

## How the flow works now

Describe the important control or data flow in execution order. Prefer a short sequence such as `request -> service -> query -> response` before introducing architecture terms.

## Important files

For each meaningful file, state what changed and what responsibility that file has. Skip generated files and boilerplate unless they matter to understanding.

## Failure and edge cases

Describe realistic failure paths or boundary cases and how the implementation handles them. Do not invent risks that are not supported by the diff.

## Technical concepts worth knowing

Include only concepts that materially help the developer read or maintain this change. Explain the behavior first, then name the technical term. Do not test vocabulary for its own sake.

## Validation

List the exact tests, commands, logs, or observations that were actually used. Do not claim checks that were not run.

<!-- Readability rule: concrete behavior -> reason -> technical term. Prefer natural teammate-to-teammate development language. Avoid translation-like wording, dense abstract nouns, and a file-by-file restatement of the full diff. -->
